{"parentUuid":"d4a9c036-91d5-4ef8-bddb-eb80d351ab24","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"6766aae3-3b42-4fb0-88eb-02234bf7486e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\\n# User Profile\\n\\n## About\\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\\n\\n## Preferences\\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\\n\\n## Working Style\\n- Direct; says \\\"stop\\\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\\\"sharpened, not soothed\\\").\\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\\n# Working Memory\\n\\n## Active Threads\\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \\\"pass builder\\\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\\n\\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":201},"type":"attachment","uuid":"eb4d62d5-169c-4994-90ba-383bd3cad507","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:31:09.835Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d7af60b9-a2bd-450d-8d80-e0f0cd6e200b","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"eb4d62d5-169c-4994-90ba-383bd3cad507","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"6766aae3-3b42-4fb0-88eb-02234bf7486e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSaturday, September 12, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Plug in the CRASHBOX SSD and run `/backup` \\u2014 uncommitted/untracked files are currently backed up nowhere.\\n- On Windows, run `/dev-sync` FIRST to pull down the encoding fix.\\n- After the next clean backup: `cp /dev/null ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` to clear the stale session-start HOOK FAILURE.\\n- Watch tomorrow's 09:00 backup \\u2014 if the index grinds again, `dev-docs` needs trimming or its own separate index (Eric's call).\\n- Decide whether the `ZoteroInjestion` submodule should track `main` instead of `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09`.\\n\\n### Session (21:41)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local work with GitHub (`erash11`) before starting a work session.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Session-start hook errors (`repo-diverged:global-memory`, `repo-diverged:dotclaude`, `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE|KnowledgeManagement|SignalPlays`) were surfaced first, then investigated \\u2014 the divergence errors had already self-cleared (both repos 0 ahead / 0 behind), and the manifest error is a backup-manifest concern, not a sync problem.\\n- Four folders initially flagged as \\\"not a repo\\\" turned out to be tracked by a parent repo \\u2014 false alarms, nothing to create.\\n- Five repos that looked missing locally were name mismatches or config repos \\u2014 nothing actually absent.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Nothing was pulled because nothing was behind: Windows last synced 9/9 and Eric has been on the Mac since. Consistent, not suspicious.\\n- Left the 17 remaining `.from-mac` / `.from-windows` files alone \\u2014 they date from the 9/9 merge, are already committed, and carry no risk.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- A folder appearing as \\\"not a repo\\\" in the DEV walk often just means it's tracked by a parent repo \\u2014 verify before running `gh repo create`.\\n- Paths with spaces (`Linda Projects`) break the parallel fetch loop in the sync script \\u2014 had to be handled as a one-off.\\n- A safety guard blocked a compound Bash command; splitting into separate calls worked.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Reconcile the 17 leftover `.from-*` files at leisure; the 9 in `graphify-out` are generated output and can most likely just be deleted.\\n- Run `/backup` if the `manifest-unmanaged` entries (`_DRIVE`, `KnowledgeManagement`, `SignalPlays`) should be addressed \\u2014 it is a backup-manifest gap, not a DEV sync gap.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"uv run python hooks/session-start.py","durationMs":218},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9b1f6169-ffa1-4136-b3a0-6d9ae61ae932","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:31:09.855Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d7af60b9-a2bd-450d-8d80-e0f0cd6e200b","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"9b1f6169-ffa1-4136-b3a0-6d9ae61ae932","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"6766aae3-3b42-4fb0-88eb-02234bf7486e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSaturday, September 12, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Plug in the CRASHBOX SSD and run `/backup` \\u2014 uncommitted/untracked files are currently backed up nowhere.\\n- On Windows, run `/dev-sync` FIRST to pull down the encoding fix.\\n- After the next clean backup: `cp /dev/null ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` to clear the stale session-start HOOK FAILURE.\\n- Watch tomorrow's 09:00 backup \\u2014 if the index grinds again, `dev-docs` needs trimming or its own separate index (Eric's call).\\n- Decide whether the `ZoteroInjestion` submodule should track `main` instead of `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09`.\\n\\n### Session (21:41)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local work with GitHub (`erash11`) before starting a work session.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Session-start hook errors (`repo-diverged:global-memory`, `repo-diverged:dotclaude`, `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE|KnowledgeManagement|SignalPlays`) were surfaced first, then investigated \\u2014 the divergence errors had already self-cleared (both repos 0 ahead / 0 behind), and the manifest error is a backup-manifest concern, not a sync problem.\\n- Four folders initially flagged as \\\"not a repo\\\" turned out to be tracked by a parent repo \\u2014 false alarms, nothing to create.\\n- Five repos that looked missing locally were name mismatches or config repos \\u2014 nothing actually absent.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Nothing was pulled because nothing was behind: Windows last synced 9/9 and Eric has been on the Mac since. Consistent, not suspicious.\\n- Left the 17 remaining `.from-mac` / `.from-windows` files alone \\u2014 they date from the 9/9 merge, are already committed, and carry no risk.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- A folder appearing as \\\"not a repo\\\" in the DEV walk often just means it's tracked by a parent repo \\u2014 verify before running `gh repo create`.\\n- Paths with spaces (`Linda Projects`) break the parallel fetch loop in the sync script \\u2014 had to be handled as a one-off.\\n- A safety guard blocked a compound Bash command; splitting into separate calls worked.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Reconcile the 17 leftover `.from-*` files at leisure; the 9 in `graphify-out` are generated output and can most likely just be deleted.\\n- Run `/backup` if the `manifest-unmanaged` entries (`_DRIVE`, `KnowledgeManagement`, `SignalPlays`) should be addressed \\u2014 it is a backup-manifest gap, not a DEV sync gap.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading knowledge base...","durationMs":223},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2b7d4693-a603-4003-a0c5-cc054d1b13f5","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:31:09.856Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d7af60b9-a2bd-450d-8d80-e0f0cd6e200b","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"2b7d4693-a603-4003-a0c5-cc054d1b13f5","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"6766aae3-3b42-4fb0-88eb-02234bf7486e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":300},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ba73730a-0cf6-475b-b2b8-19f98e10a24a","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:31:09.934Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d7af60b9-a2bd-450d-8d80-e0f0cd6e200b","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"ba73730a-0cf6-475b-b2b8-19f98e10a24a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"6766aae3-3b42-4fb0-88eb-02234bf7486e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":301},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d892b94f-35e2-4eb5-9792-a227454f00f4","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:31:09.934Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d7af60b9-a2bd-450d-8d80-e0f0cd6e200b","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"d892b94f-35e2-4eb5-9792-a227454f00f4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"6766aae3-3b42-4fb0-88eb-02234bf7486e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).","stdout":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"bash \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/scripts/check-config.sh\"","durationMs":369},"type":"attachment","uuid":"e1bf1eaa-888e-4402-abc6-5e265f687c71","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:31:10.003Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d7af60b9-a2bd-450d-8d80-e0f0cd6e200b","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"e1bf1eaa-888e-4402-abc6-5e265f687c71","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \"pass builder\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\n\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\n\n## Environment Notes\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)","## Today\nSaturday, September 12, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Plug in the CRASHBOX SSD and run `/backup` — uncommitted/untracked files are currently backed up nowhere.\n- On Windows, run `/dev-sync` FIRST to pull down the encoding fix.\n- After the next clean backup: `cp /dev/null ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` to clear the stale session-start HOOK FAILURE.\n- Watch tomorrow's 09:00 backup — if the index grinds again, `dev-docs` needs trimming or its own separate index (Eric's call).\n- Decide whether the `ZoteroInjestion` submodule should track `main` instead of `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09`.\n\n### Session (21:41)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local work with GitHub (`erash11`) before starting a work session.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Session-start hook errors (`repo-diverged:global-memory`, `repo-diverged:dotclaude`, `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE|KnowledgeManagement|SignalPlays`) were surfaced first, then investigated — the divergence errors had already self-cleared (both repos 0 ahead / 0 behind), and the manifest error is a backup-manifest concern, not a sync problem.\n- Four folders initially flagged as \"not a repo\" turned out to be tracked by a parent repo — false alarms, nothing to create.\n- Five repos that looked missing locally were name mismatches or config repos — nothing actually absent.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Nothing was pulled because nothing was behind: Windows last synced 9/9 and Eric has been on the Mac since. Consistent, not suspicious.\n- Left the 17 remaining `.from-mac` / `.from-windows` files alone — they date from the 9/9 merge, are already committed, and carry no risk.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- A folder appearing as \"not a repo\" in the DEV walk often just means it's tracked by a parent repo — verify before running `gh repo create`.\n- Paths with spaces (`Linda Projects`) break the parallel fetch loop in the sync script — had to be handled as a one-off.\n- A safety guard blocked a compound Bash command; splitting into separate calls worked.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Reconcile the 17 leftover `.from-*` files at leisure; the 9 in `graphify-out` are generated output and can most likely just be deleted.\n- Run `/backup` if the `manifest-unmanaged` entries (`_DRIVE`, `KnowledgeManagement`, `SignalPlays`) should be addressed — it is a backup-manifest gap, not a DEV sync gap.\n","## Today\nSaturday, September 12, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Plug in the CRASHBOX SSD and run `/backup` — uncommitted/untracked files are currently backed up nowhere.\n- On Windows, run `/dev-sync` FIRST to pull down the encoding fix.\n- After the next clean backup: `cp /dev/null ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` to clear the stale session-start HOOK FAILURE.\n- Watch tomorrow's 09:00 backup — if the index grinds again, `dev-docs` needs trimming or its own separate index (Eric's call).\n- Decide whether the `ZoteroInjestion` submodule should track `main` instead of `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09`.\n\n### Session (21:41)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local work with GitHub (`erash11`) before starting a work session.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Session-start hook errors (`repo-diverged:global-memory`, `repo-diverged:dotclaude`, `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE|KnowledgeManagement|SignalPlays`) were surfaced first, then investigated — the divergence errors had already self-cleared (both repos 0 ahead / 0 behind), and the manifest error is a backup-manifest concern, not a sync problem.\n- Four folders initially flagged as \"not a repo\" turned out to be tracked by a parent repo — false alarms, nothing to create.\n- Five repos that looked missing locally were name mismatches or config repos — nothing actually absent.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Nothing was pulled because nothing was behind: Windows last synced 9/9 and Eric has been on the Mac since. Consistent, not suspicious.\n- Left the 17 remaining `.from-mac` / `.from-windows` files alone — they date from the 9/9 merge, are already committed, and carry no risk.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- A folder appearing as \"not a repo\" in the DEV walk often just means it's tracked by a parent repo — verify before running `gh repo create`.\n- Paths with spaces (`Linda Projects`) break the parallel fetch loop in the sync script — had to be handled as a one-off.\n- A safety guard blocked a compound Bash command; splitting into separate calls worked.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Reconcile the 17 leftover `.from-*` files at leisure; the 9 in `graphify-out` are generated output and can most likely just be deleted.\n- Run `/backup` if the `manifest-unmanaged` entries (`_DRIVE`, `KnowledgeManagement`, `SignalPlays`) should be addressed — it is a backup-manifest gap, not a DEV sync gap.\n","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6cb87fb5-b784-423e-b2c2-044ac6ab3798","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:31:10.003Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d7af60b9-a2bd-450d-8d80-e0f0cd6e200b","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"6cb87fb5-b784-423e-b2c2-044ac6ab3798","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9e7fd7fd-e8e2-4e6f-9cdf-bc5acab40558","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**Assistant:** **Task 1 done.** The plan changed, so the Codex approval is stale — re-running the gate before touching anything.\n\n**Assistant:** Codex caught five real bugs in my amendment — including that `.gitignore` patterns ending in `/` don't match symlinks, so they'd have been re-added. Fixing all five with one explicit owned/vendor list.\n\n**Assistant:** Now the `.gitignore` patterns — the trailing slash was the bug.\n\n**Assistant:** Now the PowerShell twin needs the same vendor list, and the cron wrapper still calls the old metric name.\n\n**Assistant:** Now the metrics consumers and every count that still assumed 61.\n\n**Assistant:** Now the cron wrapper's stale key — that alone would have failed every monthly run.\n\n**Assistant:** Last: the remaining contradictory counts, and `codex-review` wrongly listed for editing.\n\n**Assistant:** Codex hit its usage limit (resets 4:46 PM). Before it died, its own grep exposed three stale lines I should fix. Let me also check whether Task 2's text changed since the approval.\n\n**Assistant:** Task 2's text is untouched since the approval. Fixing the three stale lines Codex surfaced, then running Task 2.\n\n**Assistant:** Now Task 2 — pruning the stale pool mirror. Computing the list and running both guards first.\n\n**Assistant:** Both guards clean, 98 as expected. Here's the list before I move anything.\n\n**Assistant:** That matches yesterday's audit — including the Matt Pocock duplicates the plugin already ships. Moving them out (move, not delete).\n\n**Assistant:** `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths — fair. Using Python with explicit containment checks instead.\n\n**Assistant:** 209 vs 208 — checking whether that's a hidden file `ls` didn't count.\n\n**Assistant:** Explained — a hidden `.gitignore`. Visible count is exactly 208, all 61 skills still resolve, nothing dangling. Recording and committing.\n\n**Assistant:** Task 2 done and pushed. I'm now blocked, so here's where things stand.\n\n## Done and safe\n\n**Task 1 — snapshot.** Two separate backups taken and *verified by actually restoring them*. That verification caught a real problem (below).\n\n**Task 2 — the big one.** Pruned the stale pool: **306 → 208**. The 98 skills you thought you archived yesterday are now actually gone from the folder Pi reads. Your ~150 third-party installs were not touched. All 61 skills still resolve, nothing is dangling.\n\nBoth are committed and pushed. Fully reversible.\n\n## What verifying the backup found\n\nThe restore gave **57** skills, not 61. Four were missing. Cause:\n\n```\n~/.claude/skills/claudex-loop → ~/.agents/skills/claudex-loop → ~/.agents/vendor-review/...\n```\n\nA **two-hop chain** into a vendored package — `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`. That vendor folder wasn't in my backup. Fixed, re-verified, 61/61.\n\nWorse: those four are **already committed to your `claude-skills` repo as symlinks and pushed**. On Windows they're dead links pointing at a path that doesn't exist. That's the exact failure this whole plan exists to prevent — already live, silently.\n\nDecision: they stay out of the repo, same rule as plugins. Windows needs a separate install of that package. It was already broken there; now it's explicit.\n\n## Why I stopped\n\nI amended the plan for that discovery, then re-ran your Codex gate. **Codex found 5 more bugs in my amendment** — including that my `.gitignore` lines ended in `/`, which matches folders but **not symlinks**, so the next backup run would have silently re-added all four dead links.\n\nI fixed all five. Then Codex hit its usage limit:\n\n> *You've hit your usage limit... try again at 4:46 PM*\n\nTask 3 is where the rewritten sync scripts land. Codex has now caught serious bugs in that exact code **twice**. You asked for Codex to review generated code, and I can't get that review until **4:46 PM**.\n\n## Your call\n\n- **Wait for Codex** — I resume at 4:46 PM, run the gate, then continue Tasks 3–8. Your constraint respected.\n- **Continue unreviewed now** — I run Task 3 onward on my own checks only. Faster, but that code has failed review twice already.\n\nI'd wait. The sync script is the one piece that can destroy real skills, and Codex caught a genuine data-loss bug in it on the first pass.\n\nneeds input: Codex quota is exhausted until 4:46 PM. Tell me to wait and resume then, or to continue Tasks 3–8 without the Codex review gate.\n\n**User:** use claude, that fine\n\n**Assistant:** Understood — switch the reviewer to Claude instead of Codex. That starts a fresh review session (a resume can't cross providers), so it's an independent pass on the current plan.\n\n**Assistant:** Confirmed — `--host codex` makes Claude the reviewer. Running the gate now.\n\n**Assistant:** The Claude reviewer found something Codex missed entirely, and it's bigger. Let me verify it.\n\n**Assistant:** Confirmed, and it's worse than the 4 I found. **14 of 19** outward links are third-party installs from a package manager — including 5 `mattpocock-skills` ones the plan explicitly puts out of scope. Task 4 would have copied all of them into your personal repo.\n\n**Assistant:** The picture is now clean: **42 skills Eric wrote, 19 third-party links**. Let me check this reframing before rewriting.\n\n**Assistant:** Taking the simplification. Two facts to nail down first.\n\n**Assistant:** All 19 are tracked. Four frontmatter keys in use weren't in my allowed list. Rewriting the sync script core first.\n\n**Assistant:** Now Task 4 — the inversion script is deleted entirely. Replacing that whole section.\n\n**Assistant:** API Error: Can't reach the API server — check your internet or DNS (ENOTFOUND)\n\n**User:** pause\n\n**Assistant:** Paused. Nothing is half-done: the last step was a read-only review.\n\n## Where things stand\n\n**Done and pushed:**\n- **Task 1:** snapshots taken and checked by actually restoring them.\n- **Task 2:** stale skills removed from the folder Pi reads (306 → 208). This can be undone.\n\n**Not started:** Tasks 3–8. None of your skill folders, git repos, or the daily backup have been changed beyond Task 2.\n\n**The plan:** `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`, last pushed as `00730b6`.\n\n## What the last review (round 10) found\n\nThe plan is not approved yet. Most earlier fixes held; there are three new findings:\n\n- **Serious:** the new safety hook would silently break the daily backup. The backup ignores a refused commit and still logs \"committed\". The blocked link also stays staged, so every later backup fails with no message.\n- **Minor:** some text still says `.gitignore` where it should say `.git/info/exclude`.\n- **Minor:** the Pi test user may still inherit the `staff` group, so that boundary step would stop every time.\n\nThe fix for the serious one is small: make the backup report a failed commit, and have the sync unstage any third-party link.\n\n## Big picture\n\nThe review found the plan's biggest error: **all 19 linked skills in your skills repo belong to other people** (installed packages). The plan now leaves them alone instead of copying them into your repo. Your own skills: **42**.\n\nTo continue, say **\"resume\"**. I'll fix those three findings, re-review, then start Task 3.\n\nneeds input: Paused at your request after the round-10 review (plan not yet approved; Tasks 1–2 done). Say \"resume\" to fix the three findings and continue.\n\n**User:** resume\n\n**Assistant:** Resuming. Round 10 left one serious issue and two small ones. First I'm gathering the facts each fix depends on, all at once:\n\n- how the backup handles commits\n- whether macOS nests local users into the `staff` group\n- where the stale `.gitignore` wording is"},"uuid":"ccf5415c-7917-4c81-8118-1f0e0d6caf3a","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:31:11.841Z","permissionMode":"auto","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d7af60b9-a2bd-450d-8d80-e0f0cd6e200b","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
